{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And to Moses He said, etc.</b> This passage was said to him before the Ten Commandments, on the fourth of Sivan — this is the language of Rashi. <b>And YHWH said to Moses: Come up to Me into the mount and be there</b> (Exodus 24:12) was said to him after the giving of the Torah; so too are these their words, z\"l. And the passages do not come in their sequence and not at all according to their plain meaning. Furthermore it is written (Exodus 24:3), \"And Moses came and told the people all the words of YHWH and all the ordinances\" — these are the ordinances written above, concerning which He said (Exodus 21:1), \"And these are the ordinances which you shall set before them\" — for it is not sound that this refers to the ordinances that the children of Noah were commanded and that were told to them at Marah, which they had already heard and knew. Nor would the expression \"he told\" be used except for new things that he would report (Genesis 24:66).\n\nAnd already Ibn Ezra saw well to explain the matter in its sequence, and said that until here (from above, Exodus 20:22 through 23:33) he recounted the covenant. And behold, all the passages come properly: for after the giving of the Torah, immediately on that very day, HaShem said to Moses, \"Thus shall you say to the children of Israel: You have seen that I have spoken with you from heaven\" (Exodus 20:22), and He began again to warn concerning idolatry, \"You shall not make with Me\" (Exodus 20:23), and commanded him, \"And these are the ordinances which you shall set before them,\" and all the commandments that follow thereafter, and concluded with the warning against the idolatry that they would find in the land and its worshipers (above, 23:32–33), and said to him after these commandments of yours to them: \"Come up to YHWH, you and Aaron.\" And the passage mentions that Moses did according to the commandment of YHWH, and came to the camp and told the people all the words of YHWH (Exodus 24:3) — as He had commanded him, \"Thus shall you say to the children of Israel,\" etc. (Exodus 20:19) — and all the ordinances, as He had commanded him, \"And these are the ordinances which you shall set before them\" (Exodus 21:1). And they accepted everything with joy and said, \"All that YHWH has spoken we will do.\" And the reason: all these things that HaShem has spoken to you, we will do, for we believe in your words, as he recounted in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 5:24): \"But you speak to us all that YHWH our Elohim will speak to you, and we will hear and do.\" Then Moses wrote them down (Exodus 24:4).\n\nAnd behold, Moses wrote on that day in the book everything he had been commanded — statutes and ordinances and laws — and rose early the next morning, on the morrow of that day, to make a covenant with them concerning everything, and built the altar (Exodus 24:4), and slaughtered the offerings (Exodus 24:5), and put half the blood on the altar of YHWH and half the blood he set in basins (Exodus 24:6), and took the book that he had written the previous evening and read it in their ears, and they accepted upon themselves again to enter into a covenant with Him, and said (Exodus 24:7): All that YHWH has spoken we will do and we will hear — that is, we will obey from everything that you command on His behalf, may He be exalted. Then he cast upon them half the blood (Exodus 24:8), for this is the sign of the covenant: both parties enter into equal shares. And after he completed his actions with them, it was necessary to fulfill the word of YHWH that YHWH had said to him: Come up, you and Aaron, etc. And this is what it says (Exodus 24:9): \"And Moses went up, and Aaron, Nadav and Avihu,\" etc. He also did what he had been commanded (Exodus 24:2): \"and he alone shall come near to YHWH\" — and that is what it says (Exodus 24:18): \"And Moses entered into the midst of the cloud,\" and so forth. And behold, the act of the covenant was on the day after the giving of the Torah, and on that day was the ascent that Moses made up the mountain (Exodus 24:13), and from it he was detained there forty days. And this is what the verse explains (Exodus 24:16): \"And He called to Moses on the seventh day,\" etc., and it says (Exodus 24:18): \"And Moses entered into the midst of the cloud.\" And all this is well established and clear.\n\nAnd I saw in the Mekhilta (above, 19:10) that they disagreed about the matter: some said it was before the giving of the Torah, on the fifth day, and He said to them: You are bound, held, and garnished — tomorrow come and accept upon yourselves all the commandments. And Rabbi Yosei son of Rabbi Yehudah said: On that very day all the actions were done — that is, on that day, after the giving of the Torah, the actions of addressing the people and the writing of the Book of the Covenant were done, all as we have explained. And to this one we listen, for he spoke as the halakhah:" "The reason for <b>\"And to Moses He said\"</b> is that until now the commandments and ordinances were directed to the children of Israel. Now He said that this statement was to Moses, for it is a commandment that he himself must perform — for He commanded him: after you set before them the commandments and the ordinances and make a covenant with them, \"Come up to Me.\" Therefore he performed the first commandment on the sixth day, and rose early on the seventh day in the morning and made the covenant with them, and afterward went up the mountain — he and those who were called — in the manner that they were commanded." "The reason for <b>\"Come up to YHWH\"</b> — in the way of peshat, a proper name is mentioned in place of a pronoun, as in \"the wives of Lamech\" (Genesis 4:23), \"and Jephthah, and Samuel\" (1 Samuel 12:11), and likewise \"and cause Your face to shine upon Your sanctuary that lies desolate, for HaShem's sake\" (Daniel 9:17). But in the Talmud (Sanhedrin 38b) they asked: it should say \"Come up to Me\"! Therefore they said: this is Metatron, whose name is like the name of his Master — that is, \"And to Moses He said\": HaShem, who is mentioned at the beginning of the matter, \"And YHWH said to Moses\" (Exodus 20:22) — \"come up to Metatron, who is called by My name YHWH.\" And the meaning: come up to the place of glory where the great angel is, and the intent is that Moses would enter into the midst of the cloud where the glory of YHWH is, and would not come to HaShem the singular One, for \"no human can see Me and live\" (Exodus 33:20). And the intent of our Sages is not at all as Rashi wrote above (23:21); also in tractate Sanhedrin (there) the Rav reversed the matter. And I have already mentioned (above, 12:12; 23:21) their intent regarding this name, and all their words are truth, except that they spoke in that aggadah as one who hides the face — for Rav Idi did not reveal to that heretic who was asking the nature of the great Metatron and his secret, perish the thought, but rather hinted to him that it is of the angel who guides in the lower world that this verse speaks. Therefore he said to him: even if one is aggrieved, we did not accept him, as it is written (Exodus 33:15), \"If Your face does not go\" — that we did not accept any emissary other than HaShem the honored One. And I have already explained (Ramban on Exodus 20:3) the secret of the Face and the entire matter in the explanation for the enlightened among them at the giving of the Torah:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The parenthetical verse-range note (מלעיל כ כב עד כג לג) is rendered as 'from above, Exodus 20:22 through 23:33.' The Aramaic phrase in the Mekhilta citation (קשורים תפוסים וענובים) is rendered literally, following the idiom as it stands.", 2 "The Aramaic phrase דאפילו בפרווקנא לא קבלניה (Sanhedrin 38b) is rendered hyper-literally as 'even if one is aggrieved, we did not accept him,' preserving the difficulty of the idiom. The Rav referenced is Maimonides, who in Guide for the Perplexed reverses the Talmudic reading; כמסתיר פנים is rendered 'as one who hides the face.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:03.876923402Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}